choice("Continue", B2-Ch07a-Overload, v_current_scene = B2-Ch07a-Overload)


ID: B2-Ch07a-Overload
TEXT:

 "What?..." Daren blinks. "Yes, the third-edition matrix..."

"What is Barry doing?" Kate asks, watching me flip through the notebook pages.

"He found how Eiden bypassed Daren's protections," Hadrik explains, leaning back against the stall timber. "Ward overload. Two defensive matrices interfering with each other and tearing a hole in the perimeter. Now Barry is hunting for the clash."

"Ward overload?" Kate says, a faint smirk touching her lips. "That sounds entirely like Daren."

"Found it!" I turn the notebook toward them, graphite diagrams catching the sunlight. "The sleep-dampening harmonic built into your third-edition shield matches the frequency of your standalone sleep ward almost line for line. Look at the flow vectors."

Daren squints at the open page, head tilted. "Are you certain that isn't just your abysmal handwriting? If your diagrams resemble your notes, I wouldn't trust them to map a garden path."

I stare at him. "Fine. Let's run a live test. Who knows a sleep weave?"

"I know a sleep spell," Illuna's dry voice speaks through Flower. "It requires physical contact and a few seconds of focus, but it will suffice."

"Excellent," I say. "Here are the diagrams. Aim the weave directly into the harmonic intersection where the two frequencies cross."

"I understand how ward resonance works," Illuna says. She glances once at the page and walks toward Daren.

"This should be amusing," Daren mutters, squaring his shoulders.

Illuna presses her palm against Daren's right arm, eyes closing as she tracks the aura currents.

"See?" Daren says after five seconds of stillness. "I told you there was no clash to expoi—"

Daren's eyes roll back into his skull. His knees buckle, and he hits the packed dirt like a felled timber.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch07a-Oaf, v_current_scene = B2-Ch07a-Oaf)


ID: B2-Ch07a-Oaf
TEXT:

 "That settles it," Illuna says, stepping back. "The oaf was running conflicting ward matrices."

"Well, look at that," Hadrik chuckles, resting his elbows on his knees. "Barry had the right of it. Someone ought to wake him, though. Face down in the dust cannot be good for his armor."

"Allow me," Illuna says.

A shimmer of blue light gathers above Daren's head, materializing a bucket's worth of frigid water that drops squarely onto his face.

"What in the—" Daren scrambles upright, spitting grit and coughing water from his throat. "How did I end up on the ground?"

"Take a guess," Kate says dryly.

"No..." Daren stares at his wet gauntlets. "It can't be..."

"Well, Daren," I say, closing the notebook with a snap. "Welcome to the ranks of those without physical protection magic. We have jackets."

"No!" Daren protests, wiping muddy water from his forehead scar. "I am not dropping the shield! It took two years to master that matrix. I'll surrender the standalone sleep ward before I give up the shield."

"Suit yourself," I say. "The sleep harmonic in the shield is slightly less resilient than a dedicated ward, but it leaves no gap."

"I don't care," Daren insists, wringing out his cloak. "The shield stays."

"As long as the frequencies don't cross," I say.

I pause, looking around the circle.

"Which leaves me as the only one here without a shred of sleep resistance..."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch07a-Dizziness, v_current_scene = B2-Ch07a-Dizziness)


ID: B2-Ch07a-Dizziness
TEXT:

 "Technically, I carry no magical protection either," Hadrik says. "Giants are naturally immune to sleep spells."

"Full lessathi are immune as well," Kate adds.

"They are?" I say. "What about half-lessathi?"

"No idea," Kate says. "Perhaps your resistance is halved."

"Halved?" I look at Illuna. "Can you test a sleep weave on me? Let's see what happens."

Illuna sighs, steps forward, and presses her fingers against my forearm.

Three seconds pass. Then the cobblestones lurch violently beneath my boots. The sky tilts forty-five degrees, colors smearing into a sickening blur. Nausea slams through my gut, and my balance vanishes entirely.

"Damn it!" I gasp, hands clamping onto my temples as the world spins like a tavern wheel. "Might as well be asleep for all the good this does in a fight. I can't tell up from down. Am I still standing?"

"Not even close," Hadrik's voice rumbles from somewhere above. "You're flat on your backside in the dirt holding your skull."

A cool hand touches my forehead. The spinning slows, the nausea receding like water draining from a basin. I blink, and Illuna stands over me, withdrawing her hand.

"What was that?" I ask, catching my breath.

"A counter-weave for the vertigo," Illuna says. "I can cast a ward against dizziness over you if you wish. Given your partial resistance, preventing disorientation should neutralize sleep weaves and balance-disruption spells alike. There is a trade-off, however. The ward clarifies mental focus, which leaves you significantly more vulnerable to suggestion magic. The weave lasts several weeks, though recasting every few days guarantees stability."

choice(""Alright then. Go ahead and cast the spell!"", B2-Ch07a-Adjustments, v_current_scene = B2-Ch07a-Adjustments, v_b2_ch7_dizziness = 0)
choice(""I'll pass. I really don't like the idea of being vulnerable against suggestion magic."", B2-Ch07a-Adjustments, v_current_scene = B2-Ch07a-Adjustments, v_b2_ch7_dizziness = 1)


ID: B2-Ch07a-Adjustments
TEXT:

#if(v_b2_ch7_dizziness == 0) {
 "Alright then. Go ahead and cast the spell!"

Illuna nods, weaving a short vocal cadence as she raises both palms. A pale, cool radiance washes over my shoulders for a heartbeat before dissolving into my skin.

"Done," Illuna says.

"Excellent," I say, dusting off my trousers as I stand. "It would be even better if our party's white mage could cast protective wards on anyone other than himself. You know, the foundational function of every white mage in existence?"

"Give me peace!" Daren groans, shaking his head. "I told you. Self-only matrices required half the study time. I never planned to travel in a company, so mastering team-wide ward dispersal seemed pointless when my solo barrier was more than enough to shelter civilians from monster attacks. I cannot simply tweak the formulas on the fly—I would need to drill entirely new incantations from scratch, and we have no time for an academy semester. Drop it."

"I'm only saying," I tell him, "if you hadn't split your focus between enchantment and white magic, this wouldn't be an issue."

}
#if(v_b2_ch7_dizziness == 1) {
 "I'll pass. I really don't like the idea of being vulnerable against suggestion magic."

"Suit yourself," Illuna says, lowering her hands.

"Pity we lack a white mage who can cast clean protections without drawbacks," I say, getting to my feet and dusting off my trousers. "Or rather, a white mage who can cast them on anyone besides his own frame. You know, the standard purpose of the discipline?"

"Give me peace!" Daren groans, shaking his head. "I told you. Self-only matrices required half the study time. I never planned to travel in a company, so mastering team-wide ward dispersal seemed pointless when my solo barrier was more than enough to shelter civilians from monster attacks. I cannot simply tweak the formulas on the fly—I would need to drill entirely new incantations from scratch, and we have no time for an academy semester. Drop it."

"I'm only saying," I tell him, "if you hadn't split your focus between enchantment and white magic, this wouldn't be an issue."

}
 "Drop it," Daren says firmly.

"Fine, fine," I say. "Are we finished here? Should we move?"

"One moment," Kate says, holding up a leather rucksack. "Leila's pack needs an enchantment for weight reduction. We aren't hauling these bundles by hand across Thilias."

"Right," Daren says, taking the bag. "Give it here. A basic lightening weave takes two minutes."

While Daren sketches the glyphs into the leather and Leila packs her spare clothes, we clear out of the bazaar and make our way toward the palace district. As we walk, we brief Kate and Leila on Eiden's arrival and the terms of the private conversation during the frozen seconds.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch07a-Clashing, v_current_scene = B2-Ch07a-Clashing)


ID: B2-Ch07a-Clashing
TEXT:

 "Eiden promised to grant you power to avert the calamity?" Daren frowns, glancing back at me as we climb the palace avenue. "That makes no sense. He told us to our faces that he would destroy the cities himself."

"That wasn't his phrasing," I say. "His exact words were that the destruction would happen as a direct consequence of his actions."

"Isn't that splitting hairs?" Hadrik asks, chewing on a dried strip of salted beef.

"Not at all," I say. "The destruction isn't his goal—it's collateral damage from whatever he intends to execute. Suppose Eiden knows he will be forced to battle Tyrath before the tournament wraps up. If the dragon's threats hold any truth, an all-out clash between a stillwater and an ancient dragon would tear the continent apart and flatten every city in Varathia."

"Damn," Daren mutters, jaw tightening. "You might have the right of it. With Tyrath declaring war on the settlements and Eiden declaring himself the Creator, a clash between the two leaders before the final trials is almost inevitable."

"There's more," I add. "Eiden specified the catastrophe would occur shortly before the tournament concludes. That means he expects the tournament itself to trigger the confrontation."

"Perhaps the dragon is waiting for contestants to thin out before unleashing his horde?" Hadrik suggests.

"Very likely," I say. "The cities have their own garrisons, and the beasts in the wilderness won't welcome dragon-kin either. Tyrath gains nothing by launching an assault while hundreds of foreign mages are wandering the roads, capable of butchering whole raiding parties."

choice("Continue", B2-Ch07a-Defeatist, v_current_scene = B2-Ch07a-Defeatist)


ID: B2-Ch07a-Defeatist
TEXT:

 "We should alert the king," Daren says. "He may want to begin fortifications, in case the worst comes to pass."

"Perhaps," I say. "Though I doubt stone ramparts and a handful of aerial craft will turn the tide if a stillwater and a dragon start leveling mountains."

I let out a breath, looking up at the high castle spires.

"I have a feeling we'll be dealing with that dragon ourselves if we want any judges left breathing to award the prize at the finish."

"Listen to you," Daren grins, clapping a heavy gauntlet onto my shoulder. "Are you planning to slay the dragon single-handed too, alongside saving every city on the map? You've got a full road ahead of you, I'll tell you that!"

"Shut up," I mutter. "I spoke on impulse because Eiden's posturing got under my skin. Fortunately, I happen to travel with a legendary white mage who might lend a hand if the fire gets too hot. His warding theory is atrocious and his shield needs work, but he knows which end of a broadsword cuts."

"Is that how you petition a renowned champion for aid?" Daren's grin widens. "Where is the bow of respect? Where is the humble pleading? Remember, you are addressing one of the great protectors of the continent."

choice("Bow my head and ask for help.", B2-Ch07a-Bow, v_current_scene = B2-Ch07a-Bow)
choice(""If the price of your help is theater, I'll keep the cities and skip the bow."", B2-Ch07a-Myself, v_current_scene = B2-Ch07a-Myself)
choice(""Hey, you're the hero, not me. Isn't saving cities supposed to be your job?"", B2-Ch07a-Job, v_current_scene = B2-Ch07a-Job)
choice(""You know what? On second thought, screw the cities."", B2-Ch07a-Screw, v_current_scene = B2-Ch07a-Screw)


ID: B2-Ch07a-Bow
TEXT:

 I bow my head, offering a theatrical dip of the chin.

"Please, Master Daren," I say, pitching my voice in mock solemnity. "Will you grant me your aid?"

"Whoa, whoa," Daren laughs, waving his hands. "I was jesting, Barry! You know my humor by now. If preserving Varathia's cities matters that much to you, we will see it done."

"In any case," Kate says, her voice cutting cool and sharp, "we shouldn't place too much faith in Eiden's offer of power. The conditions are deliberately vague, and Eiden breaks whim on a coin toss."

"Agreed," Daren nods. "Given how little we know of the calamity, the only certain prevention is neutralizing both Eiden and the dragon—and neither will go down quietly."

"Don't worry," I say, tapping my notebook. "I'm cataloging Eiden's loopholes. I'll have the rest unraveled in time. I didn't spend twenty years studying magical mechanics for nothing."

"Hah!" Arraka's muffled laugh echoes from the golden casing. "Now that is a spectacle I'd pay to watch: Eiden undone by his own parlor tricks."

"Let's not get ahead of ourselves," Daren cautions. "Barry deduced two mechanical flaws. A six-century stillwater likely commands hundreds. But we have reached the palace gates. Let's finish our business with the crown."

We pass through the outer portcullis into the palace courtyard. The king's servant waits at the entrance, bowing respectfully before leading us into the throne room. Golmyck is on his knees behind the dais, spanner in hand, adjusting the bronze pneumatic pistons beneath the throne.

choice("Continue", B2-Ch07a-Intervene, v_current_scene = B2-Ch07a-Intervene)


ID: B2-Ch07a-Myself
TEXT:

 "If the price of your help is theater, I'll keep the cities and skip the bow."

"Come now, don't be sour!" Daren chuckles. "You know I was only teasing. Still, you might want to balance your priorities. Getting tangled in city politics is going to cost us time while the other contestants march straight toward the trials."

"I'll manage both," I tell him.

"Naturally you will," Daren says with dry sarcasm.

"In any case," Kate says, her voice cutting cool and sharp, "we shouldn't place too much faith in Eiden's offer of power. The conditions are deliberately vague, and Eiden breaks whim on a coin toss."

